						</li>
						<li>
							<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">How long to read manga?</strong> A standard 200-page manga volume has far fewer words per page (~45–60 words) than a novel, so an average reader finishes one volume in 35 to 45 minutes (around 12–15 seconds per page).
						</li>
					</ul>

					<p class="mt-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Listening to the audio edition instead? Compare narration speeds with our <a href="/audiobook-speed-calculator/" class="font-medium text-ink underline decoration-hairline underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:decoration-ink">Audiobook Speed Calculator</a>.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- Editorial Content: How to Check Reading Time in Word, Google Docs & Kindle -->
		<section
			aria-labelledby="how-to-check-reading-time-apps"
			class="mt-10 md:mt-12"
		>
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 sm:px-6">
				<div class="max-w-3xl">
					<h2
						id="how-to-check-reading-time-apps"
						class="text-[26px] leading-[34px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.8px] text-ink sm:text-[30px] sm:leading-[38px]"
					>
						How to Check Reading Time in Word, Google Docs & Kindle
					</h2>

					<p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Neither Microsoft Word nor Google Docs displays an automatic reading time badge out of the box, but both show your exact word count so you can calculate reading duration in seconds. Amazon Kindle, by contrast, tracks your personal reading speed automatically and shows live time estimates at the bottom of your screen.
					</p>

					<h3
						class="mt-10 text-[20px] leading-[28px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.4px] text-ink sm:text-[22px] sm:leading-[30px]"
					>
						Step-by-Step Guide by App & File Type
					</h3>

					<ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body marker:text-mute">
						<li>
							<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Microsoft Word:</strong> Look at the bottom-left status bar for your total word count (or press Ctrl + Shift + G on Windows / Cmd + Shift + G on Mac). Enter that number into the Word count tab of our free online reading time calculator above—or divide your word count by 238 for silent reading and 183 for read-aloud time.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Google Docs:</strong> Open your document and press Ctrl + Shift + C (Cmd + Shift + C on Mac), or click Tools &gt; Word count from the top menu. Copy the word count into our calculator, or paste your draft directly into the Paste text tab for instant silent and spoken estimates.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Amazon Kindle:</strong> Tap the bottom-left corner of your Kindle screen while reading any eBook to cycle through display modes until you see "Time left in chapter" or "Time left in book." Kindle calculates this dynamically from your actual page-turn speed on that specific book.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">PDF Files & Web Articles:</strong> Open the PDF, press Ctrl + A (Cmd + A) to select all text, copy it (Ctrl + C), and paste it into Wordora's Paste text box—or enter the PDF's page count in the Pages tab using 450–500 words per page for dense single-spaced layouts. For blog posts and websites, paste the article text directly to see whether it hits the ideal 5-to-7-minute reader sweet spot.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Other Languages (such as Chinese, Spanish, or French):</strong> Standard English WPM benchmarks apply best to alphabetic languages like English, Spanish, and French. For character-based languages like Chinese, average adult reading speed is typically 400 to 600 characters per minute rather than space-separated words.
						</li>
					</ul>

					<p class="mt-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Need a detailed breakdown of words, characters, sentences, and paragraphs before timing your draft? Open our <a href="/word-counter/" class="font-medium text-ink underline decoration-hairline underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:decoration-ink">Word Counter</a>.
					</p>
